BUILD-DISCIPLINE candidate pattern: "a probe that can match itself always reports success." Found the hard way 2026-07-28 during gardien's first real backup deployment. Monitoring a detached remote job with `ssh host 'pgrep -f "python3 gardien.py"'` reports RUNNING forever, because the pattern appears verbatim in the command line of the very shell executing pgrep -- so the probe matches itself. Consequence in this session: a watcher loop `until ! pgrep...; do sleep 120; done` never exited, and I reported to Zach in a /cloture close that the backup was "still running at 21G" when it had in fact completed at 162G with rsync_returncode 0 some time earlier. The failure mode is the dangerous direction: it does not error, it silently asserts the liveness/busy state you were testing for, indefinitely. Fixes: a bracket pattern (`pgrep -f "python3 [g]ardien.py"`), `pgrep -f ... | grep -v $$`, or -- best -- do not probe liveness at all when the job writes a completion artifact, and check the artifact instead (gardien writes .gardien-snapshot-complete containing finished_at + rsync_returncode; reading that was what actually settled it). Why this belongs in BUILD-DISCIPLINE rather than one project's FOCUS: it is the same family as the existing "no privileged probe silencing stderr (2>/dev/null turns denied into clean)" pattern -- a check whose failure mode is to fabricate a reassuring answer rather than to fail loud. It also generalizes past pgrep to any self-referential check: `ps aux | grep foo`, `history | grep`, a lock-file probe whose own wrapper creates the lock, and -- relevant to more than one project here -- `check-project-busy`, where an interactive session's own marker is indistinguishable from a foreign one unless you compare pids against your own ancestry (cloture already warns about this; the underlying pattern is the same and is currently named in only one place). Suggested one-line form: "Does this check match itself? A self-matching probe reports the state it was testing for, forever." Filed by realisateur /ideate+/cloture session; gardien's own instance is recorded at gardien a5de01f.
